.Dd September 9, 2026
.Dt BOSD 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bosd_show ,
.Nm bosd_clear ,
.Nm bosd_alive ,
.Nm bosd_req_init ,
.Nm bosd_set_instance ,
.Nm bosd_get_instance ,
.Nm bosd_version
.Nd client library for the bosd on-screen display engine
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libbosd
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In bosd.h
.Ft void
.Fn bosd_req_init "struct bosd_req *req"
.Ft int
.Fn bosd_set_instance "const char *name"
.Ft const char *
.Fn bosd_get_instance "void"
.Ft int
.Fn bosd_alive "const char *channel"
.Ft int
.Fn bosd_show "const char *channel" "const struct bosd_req *req"
.Ft int
.Fn bosd_clear "const char *channel"
.Ft const char *
.Fn bosd_version "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm libbosd
sends display requests to a warm
.Xr bosd 1
channel daemon over a Unix datagram socket.
Callers link with
.Fl lbosd
.Pq or Cm pkg-config --libs bosd
and never need to fork the
.Nm bosd
binary.
The library does not open an X11 display; the daemon owns the overlay.
.Pp
.Fa channel
selects the daemon instance
.Pq the same name as
.Nm bosd Fl n ;
.Dv NULL
or an empty string means
.Dq default .
.Pp
.Fn bosd_req_init
clears
.Fa req
and applies the same defaults as the CLI
.Pq 2 s hold, scale 1, outline on, no bar, bar hold 3 s .
Fill the fields that apply, then call
.Fn bosd_show .
String fields accept the same backslash escapes documented in
.Xr bosd 1 .
.Pp
.Fn bosd_show
returns 0 when the datagram is accepted, or \-1 on failure
.Pq typically no warm daemon .
It does not paint locally.
.Fn bosd_clear
hides the artwork and bar on the channel.
.Fn bosd_alive
returns 1 when a daemon owns the channel.
.Pp
.Fn bosd_set_instance
and
.Fn bosd_get_instance
maintain the process-wide channel name used by the
.Nm bosd
CLI
.Pq Fl n ;
library callers normally pass
.Fa channel
explicitly instead.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn bosd_show ,
.Fn bosd_clear ,
and
.Fn bosd_set_instance
return 0 on success and \-1 on failure.
.Fn bosd_alive
returns 1 or 0.
.Fn bosd_version
returns a static string such as
.Dq 4.0 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bosd 1 ,
.Xr pkg-config 1
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Devin Teske Aq Mt dteske@FreeBSD.org
